Linear Algebra Done Right by Sheldon Axler (@math-source-operators: 0, is #reference) { r_00F8D5FB7EBA74EA7A7E1AEA27
Sheldon Axler, **Linear Algebra Done Right**, fourth edition, Springer, 2024. The inspected English PDF revision is dated **30 October 2026** on the author's page and title page. [Author page](https://linear.axler.net/) · [Authorized English PDF](https://linear.axler.net/LADR4e.pdf) · [Accessible HTML edition](https://ladr.plus/), listed separately as 21 September 2026. Do not assume the HTML revision is byte-identical to the PDF.

The fourth edition is openly available under **CC BY-NC 4.0**, with the copyright page's stated third-party exceptions. Retain attribution and follow applicable component rights; noncommercial reuse is a licence condition, not the same as unrestricted redistribution. Keep the book, figures and other source media external. No author code is included in the original teaching programs.

**Inspection — 1 November 2026:** read author/edition/access metadata, PDF title/copyright/contents, definitions 6.2–6.3 and related convention discussion (printed 183–185), orthogonality/decomposition and Cauchy–Schwarz passages 6.10–6.14 (187–189), the finite-dimensional chapter-7 assumptions and adjoint definitions/examples/properties 7.1–7.5 (227–230), self-adjoint definition/eigenvalue discussion 7.10–7.12 (233), and unitary definition/characterizations/eigenvalue modulus 7.51–7.54 (260–262). The actual PDF was inspected as text. The bounded common reconstruction uses example 7.2's rectangular real map; the complex involution examples are original.

**Progressive reading:** use chapters 1–3 to revisit complex spaces, bases and linear maps alongside the existing common foundations; then work sections 5A, 6A–6B, 7A and the unitary portion of 7D in that order. Compare proofs and independently attempt exercises before consulting answers. These complete sections and exercises are an assignment, not a claim that every passage or solution was inspected. General spectral/decomposition theory in later portions belongs to branch development with its full prerequisites.

Axler's inner product is linear in the first slot. The common operator block declares the opposite, physics-style convention and translates coefficients explicitly. The finite-dimensional hypotheses must not be silently exported to unbounded or infinite-dimensional operators. Neither the book's mathematical results nor the common examples establish quantum measurement postulates or empirical validity.

